<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dublin</h1><p class="st-meta">853 CE – 1171 CE (approx.) · capital Dublin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Norse kingdom founded around Dublin's harbour, a hub of the Viking trade network that linked Ireland to Scandinavia, Britain and the slave markets.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> built a fortified base, the longphort, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 841. From 853 the annals record <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amlaib-conung">Amlaib</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Imar</a>, ancestors of a dynasty of kings who ruled both Dublin and, at times, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-york">Kingdom of York</a>. Expelled by the Irish in 902, they returned in 917.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> was a trading town of streets, houses and workshops, revealed by excavations at Wood Quay, dealing in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and craft goods. It minted coins from about 997. Its rulers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 10th century and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitric-silkbeard">Sitric Silkbeard</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christ-church-cathedral-dublin">Christ Church Cathedral</a> around 1030. Irish kings often controlled it as a valuable prize.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clontarf">Clontarf</a> in 1014, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> was dominated by Irish overkings. In 1170 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a> captured it, and in 1171 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ireland">Lordship of Ireland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-dublin.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Dublin" width="292" height="274"><figcaption>Image: Yorkshirian · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viking_Ireland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>853 CE – 1171 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dublin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amlaib Cuaran, Sitric Silkbeard, Imar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism; Christianity from the 10th century</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse, Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Norse Kingdom of Dublin, Dyflin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dublin" aria-label="Kingdom of Dublin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4541 343 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Dublin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4861 599L4890 534M4861 599L4976 582M4861 599L4897 579M4861 599L4854 620M4861 599L4861 599"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4890 534h0M4976 582h0M4897 579h0M4854 620h0M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 599h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dublin. <a href="/atlas?year=1012&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dublin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

